item.description:
I've made a stan model in cmdrstan. In it I've added a decay function. I'm currently doing it as lambda[i] instead of a global decay function ie just lambda. After i fitted the model to my data the first time, my lambda posterior came out as bimodal, struggling with the fact that there's too much individual variation in how much decay matters (long or slow importance). I've posted an excerpt from the model part with my decay function.

I'm just unsure if I can model it as a normal distribution, rather than an exponential. I have at least not done that before, and it feels wrong, however my model is not taking forever to fit right now. If anyone has any ideas of how to solve my lambda issue, please let me know!

    model {
      // Priors --------------------------------
     // Baseline tendency: omega ~ N(1, 1)
      omega ~ normal(1, 1);

      // (More conservative prior to avoid model exploding in my face when fitting)
      A_raw ~ normal(0, 0.5);

      // Noise in observed call timing: sigma ~ truncated normal, for same above reason
      sigma ~ normal(0, 1) T[0.01,];

      // Decay rate for time-based influence (positive exponential prior) - trying a normal
      // since chains were still struggling to mix during sampling
      //lambda ~ exponential(1);
      lambda ~ normal(1, 0.5) T[0, ];

      // Likelihood --------------------------------
      for (n in 2:N_events) { // Starts at call 2, because it's alwats a comparison to previous
      // ones, can't compare to nothing!  
        int i = caller[n]; // Individual calling at time[n]
        real influence_sum = 0; // Accumulated influence on individual[i]

        // You look at all earlier call events (m &lt; n). Loop through all previous events (before  

        // current event n). See if any earlier calls (influence of these) affect currnt ones call
        for (m in 1:(n - 1)) { 
          if (bout_id[m] == bout_id[n]) { // Seperate into bouts. Only include calls from the  same bout
            int j = caller[m]; // Individual[j] who called at time[m]
            real delta_t = time[n] - time[m];  // Time since previous call

            if (delta_t &gt; 0) {
            // Use individual-specific decay rate lambda[i]
              real decay = exp(-lambda[i] * delta_t);

            // Accumulate influence from j to i, decayed by i's memory
              influence_sum += A[i, j] * decay;
            }

I've tried changing my fitting parameters around. The current setup is with a slightly wild adept_delta, from 0.99 to 0.999, and 1000 for warmup and sampling, instead of 2k


    fit &lt;- mod$sample(
      data = stan_data_g3,
      chains = 4,
      parallel_chains = 4,
      threads_per_chain = 4,
      iter_warmup = 1000,
      iter_sampling = 1000,
      adapt_delta = 0.999,
      max_treedepth = 20,
      refresh = 5,
      seed = 1990
      )
